people  do a very good job at finding patterns  in things especially things that they're  not familiar with and today since it's  valentine's day we're looking at  astronomical objects that have a shape  of a heart  so you might not think it but on the  surface of mars we've actually found  quite a few hearts these are  various images that were taken from the  mars global surveyor  on the surface so in each of these you  can actually see  clearly the shape of a heart most of  them are dips so they're actually  depressions in the surface so in this  one you can see clearly this heart  structure and it's a depression in the  ground and if the light's coming from  the left you can see the cliff right  here has a dark side and it's  eliminating this side of the of the  cliff  this one i particularly like because  it's a crater with a heart that's in the  middle of the crater so the crater goes  down into the surface and then the heart  actually pops up from the center so it  forms a mesa this is an example of how  human beings love to find patterns in  anything that they can just like when we  stare up at the sky on a sunny day  try to find patterns in the clouds  in astronomy the same thing kind of  happens and  interesting looking objects tend to get  nicknames  one class of interesting objects are  called nebulae and these are big clouds  of gas  in our galaxy  and they're where stars are born so we  often call these nebulae by interesting  nicknames based on what they look like  there's the hourglass the cat's eye the  owl the lagoon nebula the boomerang but  today for valentine's day we're showing  you the heart nebula  so you can see this beautiful  heart-shaped pattern here  which is glowing hydrogen gas  that gas is glowing because in the  middle of this nebula is a knot of stars  so this is one of my favorite galaxy  pairs this is the antenna galaxies  and these are actually two separate  galaxies that are coming together  in a marriage  of  the two galaxies to become one  so maybe a billion years ago these were  actually separate galaxies and so  they've been orbiting each other and  getting closer and closer and you can  see now that the two are really coming  together to form one and you can see  these  wings of of stars and gas so it's called  the antenna galaxy because they think  these are the antennae that look kind of  like a bugs antennae  and  a more updated version of this with the  hubble space telescope is this image so  it just zooms in on that central region  and you can really see this nice  romantic heart shape  okay so we're going to move away from  hearts for the moment  but i am going to show you an image that  has something to do with valentine's day  this is probably one of the most  romantic  images made  if you think of romance not just being  about love but about big ideas the  connection with valentine's day is that  20 years ago  a little spacecraft called voyager 1  shooting out to the outer reaches of the  solar system  its its mission finished to explore the  planets was commanded to turn around and  take a family portrait of our solar  system and of course the connection with  valentine's day is that it was on  february 14 1990 20 years ago that this  little spacecraft was sent the  instruction to turn around its mission  completed and start taking a picture of  our of our solar system family  and this is the picture it took it was  four billion miles away from the sun  and it went and took 60 different  pictures stitched together here in this  panorama that capture almost all of the  members of our solar system family so  you see the the sun in the center earth  and venus very close by unfortunately it  missed out mars and mercury but it got  all the rest of the planets jupiter  saturn uranus and neptune aside from the  grandeur of this whole picture put  together  um it's really one particular frame of  this that really that captured the  public's imagination  and that is of course the portrait of  earth because that's that's our home and  so if we zoom in on that one little  picture  you're seeing a picture of earth from  four billion miles away  and earth  is this little tiny dot right in the  center  so the remarkable thing about this  particular image is that it really gives  us a perspective on our place in the  solar system and just how small and  insignificant our precious little home  is  this is a tiny little dot in this one  little picture and that's earth that's  our home  but really i'm just paraphrasing a great  scientist carl sagan here because he had  some much more profound words to say  about this  he said look again at that dot  that's here that's home that's us  on it everyone you love everyone you  know  everyone you ever heard of every human  being who ever was lived out their lives  he goes on to call it a moat of dust  suspended in a sunbeam  and that's absolutely true because  it is this little dot it happens purely  by chance to be caught on some reflected  light within the camera coming from the  sun

